      "narrative": "You drop to a crouch beneath the black arch, sweeping your gaze across the packed earth. Loose dirt. Scattered dead leaves. A few small stones kicked up by boots. Nothing catches your attention \u2014 no disturbed soil, no hidden cache, no marks suggesting anything buried or stashed.\n\nYour fingers brush the cold ground. Just dirt.\n\nBehind you, the hooded figure lets out a low, dry chuckle. Not mocking \u2014 tired, like he's watched someone do this before.\n\n\"You're not the first to search that ground,\" he says. \"Won't be the last. Whatever's hidden here, it's not underfoot.\"\n\nHe turns back to the filed keyhole, tapping it once with a gloved finger. The tap rings hollow against the brass.\n\n\"The gate keeps its secrets where they can't be stepped on.\""
