      "narrative": "The Copper Finch's door groans open, and the warmth hits you first \u2014 the low breath of the hearth fire, the smell of old ale and lamp oil. The morning crowd has thinned. A few heads turn, then look away.\n\nYour old table sits in the corner by the shuttered window. Scarred oak. A groove carved into its edge from some forgotten knife. The chair scrapes against the floorboards as you pull it out and sit.\n\nA moment passes. Then Mara Venn appears from behind the bar, wiping a clay cup with a rag. She walks over without hurry, sets the cup down across from you, and slides into the opposite chair.\n\n\"Back already,\" she says. Not a question. Her sharp eyes study your face for a long second. \"You met Sable, then. Or at least saw him.\"\n\nShe leans back, the chair creaking. \"He say anything useful, or just stare at that lock like he's been doing for three winters?\""
